# graphify-out/graph.json is a large generated networkx node-link JSON blob.
# Git's default line-based text merge produces useless conflict markers on it.
# Use Orion's bounded three-way JSON union driver (registered locally via
# scripts/setup_graphify_merge_driver.sh — see that script for details). The
# driver command it configures is scripts/graphify_lfs_merge_driver.sh, an
# LFS-aware wrapper -- see that script for why a plain `graphify merge-driver`
# invocation would receive raw LFS pointer stubs instead of real content.
#
# git-LFS-tracked as of 2026-09-08 (fix/graph-json-lfs): a routine refresh
# grew this file past GitHub's hard 100MB-per-blob push limit. Migration is
# forward-only -- existing history is NOT rewritten (142 live worktrees/
# branches made that too risky), so old commits still carry the file as a
# plain blob; only commits from this branch onward store it via LFS.
# Requires `git lfs install` once per machine for a checkout of this path to
# produce real content instead of a ~130-byte pointer stub (see CLAUDE.md's
# graphify section for whether that's already global on a given box).
#
# OPERATIONAL RISK, accepted knowingly, not solved by this patch: this repo
# is public on GitHub's free LFS tier -- 1GB storage + 1GB/month download
# bandwidth, account-wide, across every repo on the account. This file alone
# is ~100MB/version and changed 6 times on main in the last 30 days before
# this migration, plus more across branches pre-merge, plus every CI
# checkout that pulls real LFS content. That budget can be exhausted by
# storage or by roughly 10 pulls in a month; once it is, further LFS
# push/pull is blocked until a paid data pack is purchased. Check current
# consumption with `git lfs env` (local view) or GitHub's own repo -> Settings
# -> Billing -> "Git LFS Data" usage page (the actual account-wide meter).
graphify-out/graph.json filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=graphify -text
